    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The area of one of the 2-by-3 rectangles is 2*(3) = 6. Since the area of the square that needs to be covered is also 6, this implies that the side length of the square is the same as the length of the 2-by-3 rectangles, or \\(\\sqrt{6}\\). Since \\(\\sqrt{6}\\) is not an integer, it cannot be the length of a rectangle or square. Because one of the dimensions of the square must be the same as the length of the rectangle, the dimensions of the square are 2 and 3\\(\\sqrt{2}\\). Since 3\\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is not an integer, it cannot be the length of a rectangle or square. Therefore, one of the dimensions of the square is 2 and the other dimension is 3\\(\\sqrt{2}\\), and the necessary number of 2-by-3 rectangles that fit to completely cover the square must be \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
