    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we first need to calculate the area of both the square and the rectangles. \n\nSince a 2-by-3 rectangle has an area of 2*3=6, we must have:\n\nArea(square)=  Area(rectangles)\n\nTherefore, to be able to completely fill the square, the area of the square should be a multiple of 6.\n\nThe smallest square that can be tiled with 2-by-3 rectangles is a square with a side length of 6. This has an area of 6 * 6 = 36.\n\n2*3 * 5 rectangles can fill this square.\nThe answer is 5",
